<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman Campagna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agro Romano</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman Campagna is the low, undulating plain surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Densely settled in antiquity, it was later given over to great estates and grazing and became malarial and thinly populated, a landscape of ruins painted by artists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-lorrain">Claude Lorrain</a> and Nicolas Poussin. Land reclamation and the control of malaria in the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries brought renewed settlement and farming.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-campagna.jpg" alt="Roman Campagna" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Stanley Haseltine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Stanley_Haseltine_-_Morning_LIght,_Roman_Campagna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
